  "source": "# The library\n\nKydux's own art, made for the game and under the GNU GPL, version 3 or\nlater, like the rest of it.\n\n- Painted by Gemini 3 Pro Image (`gemini-3-pro-image`) from\n  `tools/art/prompts/image-first-library-6.md`, with the field as its\n  style's reference, then edited by it without its grid\n  (`game-library6-nogrid.md`).\n- Its bottom mended by Gemini from `game-library-margin-3.md`, joined by\n  `tools/art/repaint.py`, the painting kept pixel for pixel elsewhere: the\n  castle's corridor below the double door, plain pillars in the corners,\n  plain wall beside the door.\n- The way to the vault ending in an iron safe, painted by Gemini from\n  `game-library-vault-2.md` in a box of the painting, joined by\n  `tools/art/repaint.py`.\n- Its sides painted on by Gemini from `image-first-library-margin.md`, three\n  cells on each side, on a canvas made and joined by `tools/art/margin.py`:\n  the castle's walls going on, and grass and trees beyond them.\n- Its door arches drawn over whoever passes under them, and its walls and\n  the little table by Eldra's armchair, cut by polygons, each edge on its\n  outline (static things).\n- Its counter, bookcases, pile of books, tables, chairs, desk and Eldra's\n  book painted flat magenta by Gemini in crops of the painting\n  (`game-library-marked-*.md`, each asked three times and voted), and cut\n  there with their outlines.\n- Each door open onto the floor of the room beyond, Gemini's edit of the\n  doorway alone (`game-library-centre-open.md`, `-wenna-open.md`,\n  `-room-open.md`).\n- Each thing that opens or is picked up cut against Gemini's edit without it\n  (`game-library-nokeys.md`, `-nobook.md`, `-nooldbook.md`); Eldra's own\n  book against the shelf's back carried over it by hand; the armchair, which\n  stands in front of whoever is behind it, against Gemini's edit without it\n  (`game-library-noarmchair.md`). The two books drawn smaller than painted.\n- Its cells marked by hand, cut by `tools/art/image_first.py` and exported by\n  `tools/art/export_scene.py`.\n",
